abstract:
The aim of this paper is to review the latest total fat intake data for South Africa, as well as scientific evidence on the&#13;
effect of the total amount and quality or type of fat in the diet. The total fat intake of South Africans is within the goal&#13;
of ≤ 30% of total energy, but the quality or type of fat in the diet requires attention. Fats are key nutrients required for&#13;
early growth and development, and influence the body’s response to nutrition-related noncommunicable diseases later&#13;
in life. Based on the total fat intake data for South Africa, and the latest evidence on the unique properties of certain&#13;
fatty acids for health and disease, revision of the previous food-based dietary guideline (FBDG), “Eat fat sparingly”, was&#13;
needed. “Use fats sparingly: choose vegetable oils, rather than hard fats” is the new FBDG formulated for the fat intake&#13;
of South Africans. Replacing animal and plant sources of saturated fatty acids with polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs)&#13;
and monounsaturated fatty acids is recommended. The regular intake of oily fish to increase omega-3 long-chain PUFAs&#13;
is important. Energy balance remains an important aspect, in addition to the composition of the diet. The FBDG “Use fats&#13;
sparingly: choose vegetable oils rather than hard fats” is meant to convey a positive message, to ensure that the right&#13;
types of fats and oils are eaten and used in food preparation for early development and long-term health. An alternative&#13;
FBDG is: “Eat and use the right type of fats and oils in moderation”
